  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nFor the rest of today and tonight across the Northland, \ngenerally quiet and seasonable conditions will prevail. High \npressure keeps our skies mostly sunny with afternoon \ntemperatures reaching the lower to mid 80s. A stray shower or \nisolated storm might pop up near the International Border this \nafternoon, but most locations will remain completely dry. \nOvernight lows will comfortably drop into the upper 50s and \nlower 60s under mostly clear skies.\n\nFriday will feature a noticeable increase in heat and humidity\nahead of an approaching cold front. As this front pushes into \nthe region, we are monitoring a severe threat for Friday \nafternoon and evening across north-central Minnesota. The \nprimary hazards with these elevated storms will be large hail up\nto ping pong ball size and damaging wind gusts. This particular\nimpulse is leftover convection that should be dying or on the \ndownhill as it moves from the Dakotas into Minnesota. Some \nstorms may hold together to pose a threat especially if they \nstay in a line.\n\nBehind the front on Saturday, conditions dry out, but the heat\nquickly builds back in as an upper-level ridge amplifies over \nthe region. Hot temperatures are showing up in some of the \nguidance for Sunday and Monday, which could push heat temperatures\nwell into the 90s and possibly the low 100s. In addition to the\nsweltering conditions, another severe threat exists again on \nSunday as a mid-level boundary sets up across the area. The \nrange in guidance for max t's likely exists as the convective \nthreat would likely steal the energy for storms rather than \nheat. That is probably the likely scenario, so not hitting the \nheat headlines too hard. There is a small cap which also \nsuggests convection may win out. It will be interesting to see \nhow the higher rez convective allowing models pan out for this.\n\nThe unseasonably hot and humid pattern will persist into early\nnext week, with Monday remaining quite hot across the Northland.\nA few lingering, isolated showers or storms are possible Monday\ninto Tuesday, but the strong upper ridge will suppress most \nwidespread activity. By the middle of next week, temperatures \nmay moderate slightly back into the 80s, but the overall pattern\nremains largely dry across the region. Although we will see \nscattered showers and storms, rainfall will be here and there, \nbut not really a good soaking one is in sight aside from if you \nget lucky from a thunderstorm.\n\nThe 6-10 day CPC outlook for the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes\nregion favors a strong probability of above normal temperatures\nas an upper level ridge dominates the central United States. \nPrecipitation across our area leans toward below normal \nprobabilities.",
